{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789793523,"nonce":"laWbM7zDpnCRQfk1JKajMLczAJE=","title":"Interpreter: a frame's own storage was invisible to the collector","message":"The interpreter allocates aggregate storage for a frame from the pool\n(undef aggregates, allocas, aggregate copies, a callee's destination) and\nkeeps the blocks in a Vec it frees when the frame ends. The Vec is a Rust\nheap buffer, so once the register that held a block was reused, or for a\ndestination the callee wrote into, nothing the collector scans named the\nblock: the sweep reclaimed it while the frame ran, and the frame's exit\nfreed it again. A block twice on a free list is a cycle, and the next\ncollection's free-list walk never ended: `import nqueens; nqueens.run()`\nhung in `collect` after its second collection, while the same program\nunder `main()` happened to collect at other moments and passed.\n\nFix: `Scratch` registers its block list as a root range and re-registers\nit whenever the Vec moves; the collector then marks the blocks and what\nthey hold.","files":null}]}